-- GitLab -->> database backed web application


GitLab is a web-based DevOps platform that helps developers manage software projects. It combines Git-based version control with tools for collaboration, automation, and deployment.

Here's what GitLab is commonly used for:

Source code management: Store and track code using Git.
Collaboration: Multiple developers can work on the same project, create branches, merge changes, and review each other's code.
Issue tracking: Manage bugs, feature requests, and project tasks.
CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment): Automatically build, test, and deploy applications whenever code changes are pushed.
Code reviews: Use merge requests (similar to GitHub pull requests) to review and discuss code before merging.
Security and monitoring: Scan code for vulnerabilities and monitor application performance.

-Distributed Git - Distributed Workflows

it refers to how Git allows multiple developers to work on their own copies of a repository and synchronize their work.
Unlike a centralized system where there is one main server containing the repository, Git gives every developer a complete copy of the repository, including its history.

             GitHub / GitLab
              Remote Repo
                  │
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐
        ↓         ↓         ↓
     Developer  Developer  Developer
       Git        Git        Git
     Repo       Repo       Repo

Each developer can work independently.
Typical Git distributed workflow
Suppose you are working on a project.
1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/user/project.git
Now you have your own complete local repository.
2. Create a branch
git checkout -b login-feature
You work on your feature without disturbing the main code.
3. Make changes and commit
git add .
git commit -m "Add login feature"

Push to remote

git push origin login-feature

Now your branch is available on GitHub.

5. Create a Pull Request

You ask the team to review and merge your changes into main.

Your computer
     │
     │ git push
     ▼
GitHub
     │
     │ Pull Request
     ▼
   Review
     │
     ▼
   main


Why is it called "distributed"?

Because Git doesn't require you to constantly communicate with the central server.
You can do many operations locally:

git log
git diff
git branch
git commit
git merge

Even without internet.

The remote repository (GitHub/GitLab) is mainly used to share and synchronize your work.

Centralized vs Distributed
Centralized VCS	Distributed Git
Central server is essential	Each developer has a full repo
Usually requires server for history operations	Most operations work offline
Server is the main source of history	Every clone has repository history
Example: SVN	Example: Git

